<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-06">
  <title>Week 6: First-order ODEs (separable, linear, exact)</title>

 <subsection xml:id="first-order-ode-notes">

<title>First-Order Ordinary Differential Equations</title>

<p>
<strong>Source:</strong> These notes are adapted from
<xref ref="lamar-first-order"/>.
</p>

<remark xml:id="lamar-first-order">
<p>
Paul Dawkins, <q>First Order Differential Equations</q>,
Paul's Online Math Notes,
<link url="https://tutorial.math.lamar.edu/classes/de/introfirstorder.aspx">https://tutorial.math.lamar.edu/classes/de/introfirstorder.aspx</link>.
</p>
</remark>

<introduction>
<p>
Differential equations arise when a quantity and its rate of change are related.
Examples include population growth, radioactive decay, cooling processes,
mixing problems, and engineering systems.
</p>

<p>
A <term>first-order ordinary differential equation</term> is an equation involving an
unknown function <m>y(t)</m> and its first derivative.
</p>

<md>

\frac{dy}{dt}=f(y,t)

</md>

<p>
Unlike algebraic equations, there is no single method that solves all
first-order differential equations. Instead, we identify the type of equation
and apply an appropriate solution technique.
</p>
</introduction>
</subsection>


<subsection xml:id="modeling">

<title>Modeling with First-Order Differential Equations</title>

<p>
Many engineering and scientific applications lead naturally to first-order
differential equations.
</p>

<examples>
<ul>
<li>Population growth and decay</li>
<li>Mixing problems</li>
<li>Radioactive decay</li>
<li>Cooling and heating processes</li>
<li>Velocity of falling objects</li>
</ul>
</examples>

<p>
The modeling process usually follows these steps:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Define the unknown quantity.</li>
<li>Identify how the quantity changes over time.</li>
<li>Write a differential equation describing the relationship.</li>
<li>Solve the differential equation.</li>
<li>Interpret the solution in context.</li>
</ol>

<example>
<title>Concrete Curing on a Bridge Deck</title>

<statement>

<p>
After a bridge deck is poured, engineers monitor the concrete temperature
to ensure proper curing. Suppose the temperature <m>T(t)</m> of the concrete
decreases at a rate proportional to the difference between the concrete
temperature and the surrounding air temperature of <m>20^\circ\text{C}</m>.
</p>
<p>
The rate of cooling is proportional to the temperature difference
between the concrete and the surrounding air.
</p>
<md>
\frac{dT}{dt}=-k(T-20),
</md>
<p>
where <m>k>0</m> is a proportionality constant.
</p>
</statement>

</example>
</subsection>


<subsection xml:id="classification">

<title>Classifying First-Order Differential Equations</title>

<p>
When presented with a differential equation, the first step is to identify its
form.
</p>

<p>
Some important classes of first-order differential equations include:
</p>

<ul>
<li>Separable equations</li>
<li>Linear equations</li>
<li>Exact equations</li>
<li>Bernoulli equations</li>
</ul>

<p>
The goal is to recognize which solution method can be applied.
</p>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="separable-equations">

<title>Separable Differential Equations</title>

<definition>
<p>
A differential equation is <term>separable</term> if it can be written in the form
</p>

<md>

N(y)\frac{dy}{dx}=M(x)

</md>

<p>
or equivalently
</p>

<md>

\frac{dy}{dx}=f(x)g(y).

</md>
</definition>

<subsubsection>

<title>Solution Strategy</title>

<ol>
<li>Move all <m>y</m>-terms to one side and all <m>x</m>-terms to the other.</li>

<li>Integrate both sides.</li>

<li>Solve for <m>y</m>, if possible.</li>

<li>Use the initial condition if one is provided.</li>
</ol>

</subsubsection>

<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>Classify and solve the differential equation</p>
        <md>
            
            \frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{x^2+1}{y(y^2+4)}.
            
        </md>
    </statement>

    <solution>
        <p>This equation is <em>separable</em>.</p>

        <md>
            
            y(y^2+4)\,dy=(x^2+1)\,dx.
            
        </md>

        <p>Integrate both sides:</p>

        <md>
            
            \int (y^3+4y)\,dy=\int (x^2+1)\,dx.
            
        </md>

        <md>
            
            \frac{y^4}{4}+2y^2=\frac{x^3}{3}+x+C.
            
        </md>

        <p>This implicit equation is the general solution.</p>
    </solution>
</exercise>


</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="difflinear-equations">

<title>Linear Differential Equations</title>

<definition>
<p>
A first-order linear differential equation has the form
</p>

<md>

y'+p(x)y=g(x).

</md>
</definition>

<p>
These equations are solved using an integrating factor.
</p>

<subsubsection>

<title>Integrating Factor</title>

<p>
For
</p>

<md>

y'+p(x)y=g(x),

</md>

<p>
the integrating factor is
</p>

<md>

\mu(x)=e^{\int p(x)\,dx}.

</md>

<p>
Multiplying the differential equation by <m>\mu(x)</m> transforms the left-hand
side into a product derivative:
</p>

<md>

\frac{d}{dx}\big(\mu(x)y\big)=\mu(x)g(x).

</md>

<p>
Integrate both sides and solve for <m>y</m>.
</p>

</subsubsection>

<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>Classify and solve the differential equation</p>
        <md>
            
            (x^2+1)\frac{dy}{dx}+2xy=\sin x.
            
        </md>
    </statement>

    <solution>
        <p>Divide by <m>x^2+1</m>:</p>

        <md>
            
            y'+\frac{2x}{x^2+1}y=\frac{\sin x}{x^2+1}.
            
        </md>

        <p>This is a <em>linear first-order differential equation</em>.</p>

        <p>The integrating factor is</p>

        <md>
            
            \mu(x)
            =e^{\int \frac{2x}{x^2+1}\,dx}
            =e^{\ln(x^2+1)}
            =x^2+1.
            
        </md>

        <p>Multiplying through by the integrating factor gives</p>

        <md>
            
            \frac{d}{dx}\big((x^2+1)y\big)=\sin x.
            
        </md>

        <p>Integrating:</p>

        <md>
            
            (x^2+1)y=-\cos x+C.
            
        </md>

        <md>
            
            y=\frac{-\cos x+C}{x^2+1}.
            
        </md>
    </solution>
</exercise>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="exact-equations">

<title>Exact Differential Equations</title>

<definition>
<p>
A differential equation written as
</p>

<md>

M(x,y)\,dx+N(x,y)\,dy=0

</md>

<p>
is exact if
</p>

<md>

\frac{\partial M}{\partial y}
=
\frac{\partial N}{\partial x}.

</md>
</definition>

<p>
When an equation is exact, there exists a potential function
<m>F(x,y)</m> such that
</p>

<md>

dF=M\,dx+N\,dy.

</md>

<p>
The solution is obtained by finding <m>F(x,y)</m> and setting
</p>

<md>

F(x,y)=C.

</md>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="bernoulli-equations">

<title>Bernoulli Differential Equations</title>

<definition>
<p>
A Bernoulli equation has the form
</p>

<md>

y'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n,

</md>

<p>
where <m>n\neq 0</m> and <m>n\neq 1</m>.
</p>
</definition>

<p>
These equations can be transformed into linear equations through the
substitution
</p>

<md>

v=y^{1-n}.

</md>

<p>
After substitution, solve the resulting linear differential equation.
</p>
<exercise>
    <statement>
        <p>Classify and solve the differential equation</p>
        <md>
            
            y'+\frac{2}{x}y=x^3y^{-2}.
            
        </md>
    </statement>

    <solution>
        <p>This is a <em>Bernoulli equation</em> with <m>n=-2</m>.</p>

        <p>Let</p>

        <md>
            
            v=y^{1-(-2)}=y^3.
            
        </md>

        <p>Then</p>

        <md>
            
            v'=3y^2y'.
            
        </md>

        <p>Multiply the differential equation by <m>3y^2</m>:</p>

        <md>
            
            v'+\frac{6}{x}v=3x^3.
            
        </md>

        <p>This is linear in <m>v</m>.</p>

        <p>The integrating factor is</p>

        <md>
            
            \mu(x)=e^{\int 6/x\,dx}=x^6.
            
        </md>

        <p>Multiply through by <m>x^6</m>:</p>

        <md>
            
            \frac{d}{dx}(x^6v)=3x^9.
            
        </md>

        <p>Integrate:</p>

        <md>
            
            x^6v=\frac{3}{10}x^{10}+C.
            
        </md>

        <md>
            
            v=\frac{3}{10}x^4+\frac{C}{x^6}.
            
        </md>

        <p>Substitute back <m>v=y^3</m>:</p>

        <md>
            
            y^3=\frac{3}{10}x^4+\frac{C}{x^6}.
            
        </md>

        <md>
            
            y=\left(\frac{3}{10}x^4+\frac{C}{x^6}\right)^{1/3}.
            
        </md>
    </solution>
</exercise>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="initial-value-problems">

<title>Initial Value Problems</title>

<definition>
<p>
An <term>initial value problem</term> (IVP) consists of a differential equation
together with an initial condition.
</p>

<md>

\frac{dy}{dx}=f(x,y),
\qquad
y(x_0)=y_0.

</md>
</definition>

<p>
The differential equation determines a family of solutions, while the
initial condition identifies one specific solution.
</p>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="euler-method-preview">

<title>Numerical Approximation: Euler's Method</title>

<p>
Not all differential equations can be solved analytically. One common
numerical technique is Euler's Method.
</p>

<p>
Starting from <m>(x_n,y_n)</m>, the next approximation is computed using
</p>

<md>

y_{n+1}
=
y_n+h\,f(x_n,y_n),

</md>

<p>
where <m>h</m> is the step size.
</p>

<p>
Euler's Method follows the tangent line to generate approximate solution
values.
</p>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="summary">

<title>Summary</title>

<p>
When solving a first-order differential equation:
</p>

<ol>
<li>Identify the type of differential equation.</li>
<li>Select an appropriate solution method.</li>
<li>Find the general solution.</li>
<li>Apply initial conditions when given.</li>
<li>Interpret the solution in context.</li>
</ol>

<p>
The most common first-order differential equations encountered in an
introductory course are separable, linear, exact, and Bernoulli equations.
Recognizing the structure of an equation is often the most important step in
finding its solution.
</p>

</subsection>

</section>
